Read it with six cautions.
* The two-point fit passes through its two endpoints BY CONSTRUCTION, so
it cannot disagree with them: its `majit fixed` is an extrapolation to
n=0 carrying no residual of its own. `mid err` is the whole test of that
model — the worst relative error at a ladder point the fit did not
touch — and a `-` means the ladder had no such point and the row is
entirely unchecked.
* `ls fixed` is the same model fitted by LEAST SQUARES over every included
point. It is the second opinion, not the better number, and it does not
replace the two-point figure: reproducing #88's METHOD is what makes a
number here comparable to one taken then. Unlike the first it touches no
point, so every point is a residual — `ls err` is the worst of them. A
`-` means fewer than three points, where least squares is just the line
through the two and its zero residual would say nothing. No interval is
printed for either: four or five points at one sample each cannot carry
one, and printing one would claim a precision this harness has not got.
* `majit fixed` is `t(n_lo)` MINUS the slope's contribution at n_lo, and
both are printed so that correction can be sized rather than assumed.
Where it is a few percent of `t(n_lo)` the intercept inherits the
precision of a DIRECTLY MEASURED point, not the model's: `mid err` and
`ls err` describe the model BETWEEN the endpoints, so they bound the
per-element term and any extrapolation past the ladder's reach, and a
large one does not make the intercept uncertain by the same fraction.
The reading that does put the intercept in doubt is the opposite one —
`per_elem * n_lo` a LARGE fraction of `t(n_lo)`, where `majit fixed` is
mostly the subtraction of a modelled quantity from a measured one.
* `excl` is ladder members whose loop never compiled, so they are NOT in
the fit: their `majit` cell is the tracing interpreter, and fitting it
would decompose a different machine. (0 across all ladders.)
It is printed PER LADDER because `points` alone cannot say why a rung
is missing: `points=2, excl=1` is a decline worth investigating, while
`points=2, excl=0` is a rung this run simply did not run. A chunked-out
rung never becomes a row at all, so it is invisible to `excl` — the two
columns are only informative together.
* `gfails/call` belongs beside `majit fixed`, not in a separate table:
#88 found 1.00 guard failure per call on every compiled case and named
it the prime suspect for the fixed cost it measured. A fixed cost read
without it is half a finding.
* `bridges/call` counts artifacts COMPILED in the warm window, not
artifacts entered. A zero says the artifact population stopped
growing, NOT that the call enters no bridge; a nonzero one says a warm
call is still compiling, which puts compilation inside `majit fixed`.

BODY-size ladder: the element count is FIXED and only the body of the `map`
varies.
case                           elems    ops  loop ops     exec ns    stock ns  exec/elem  stock/elem
map_body/x                      1000     13         -     10453.6      9437.9      10.45        9.44
map_body/x*2                    1000     13         -     20064.7     22320.8      20.06       22.32
map_body/x*2+1                  1000     15         -     34601.9     31572.2      34.60       31.57
map_body/x*2+1-3                1000     17         -     48471.4     41356.5      48.47       41.36

The three size ladders above hold the expression fixed and grow `n`, so they
price an ELEMENT and cannot say what inside an element the cost is. These hold
`n` fixed and grow the body, so the loop scaffolding every rung runs is
identical and the only thing that moves between two rungs is how many
instructions the body itself contributes.

`ops` is every instruction in the compiled stream. `loop ops` is the span that
runs once per element: the instructions from the single backward `Jump`'s
target through that `Jump`, inclusive. Both come from the COMPILER, not from
counting operators in the source — see `Ops` for why that distinction is the
point of the column and for the one caveat, that the counts are read off a
recompilation. `loop ops` prints `-` unless the stream holds exactly one
backward jump, which is a refusal to name a span rather than a guess at one.

`exec/elem` and `stock/elem` are that rung's whole call divided by `elems`. The
per-call fixed cost is NOT differenced away — there is no size ladder here to
take it out with — so each figure carries its own, spread across every element.

`stock/elem` is the CONTROL and not decoration: the same bodies through the
other evaluator, against the same x-axis. Where it is flat in body size and
`exec/elem` is not, what grows with the body is the VM's cost per instruction
and not the expression's own.
